def populate(deck):
 deck('welcome','Use this course',[
 ('Learn to organise information','By the end, you should be able to propose an information structure, choose meaningful labels, plan research, interpret finding problems and specify navigation. Reading every slide is completion; applying and testing the decisions provides stronger evidence of learning.'),
 ('Follow the reading path','Start here, then work through Principles, Research and Navigation in the order recommended by NN/g. The final applied workshops connect those ideas to an end-to-end project. All lessons remain available if you need to jump to a specific question.'),
 ('Work in small sessions','Choose one lesson and complete its practice before moving on. Use your notes to record an answer, uncertainty and next check. Return later and explain the idea without rereading. Short slides make delivery manageable; they do not guarantee understanding.'),
 ('Understand the sources','This independent course uses original explanations, fictional examples and exercises. It is not an official NN/g course or a reproduction of its publications. External readings open in a new tab. Video pages were reviewed through their available summaries; video transcripts were not available.'),
 ('Use voice as a study tool','On Vercel, private sign-in reveals the microphone icon. The tutor receives the current slide heading and text, not a view of your screen or private notes. Ask it to explain a term, challenge a decision or read the slide. API usage is billed separately.'),
 ('Keep your work','Progress and notes stay in this browser. Use the report and copy/export controls for a separate record. Practical exercises are self-assessed; quizzes check selected concepts and do not certify professional competence.')],['guide'])
 deck('cedar-brief','Choose your practice project',[
 ('Meet Cedar Community Centre','Cedar is a fictional service offering classes, events, membership, swimming, room hire and support. New visitors need to understand what exists. Returning members need quick routes to bookings, preparation, cancellation and fee information.'),
 ('Use this starting inventory','| ID | Content | Current location |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| C1 | Pottery for beginners | Activities |\n| C2 | Course dates and fees | Downloads |\n| C3 | Cancel a booking | Member zone |\n| C4 | Reduced fees | About |\n| C5 | Accessible facilities | Footer |\n| C6 | Hire a room | Other |'),
 ('Add enough variety','Include swimming age limits, tutor profiles, contact details, opening hours, events, equipment loans, membership renewal, venue directions and booking preparation. These authored items give you ambiguity and different tasks to investigate. They are not findings from actual Cedar users.'),
 ('Define success before redesign','Choose three outcomes: a newcomer finds an affordable evening class; a member checks cancellation conditions; a visitor determines whether a venue meets access needs. Write what information would demonstrate success for each.'),
 ('Record assumptions','For each task, write what you know, what you assume and what evidence you need. If using your own site, replace Cedar with a manageable area while preserving important content types and tasks.'),
 ('Save a baseline','Keep your starting structure and labels. Later you will compare them with a revised structure using equivalent tasks. Do not silently rewrite your baseline after you see the results.')],[])
 deck('inventory','Build an inventory and audit',[
 ('Create the evidence base','This authored workshop starts before rearranging menus. An inventory records the content space; an audit evaluates whether each item is useful, accurate and maintainable. Include functions, documents and externally hosted steps as well as ordinary pages.'),
 ('Record comparable fields','| Field | Example |\n| --- | --- |\n| Stable ID | C3 |\n| URL and title | /members/cancel; Cancel a booking |\n| Type and task | Policy; understand refund eligibility |\n| Owner and status | Booking team; review required |\n| Relationships | Booking record, payment help |'),
 ('Evaluate quality separately','Check evidence of a user need, accuracy, accessibility, duplication, relevance and ownership. An analytics count is a clue about use, not proof of usefulness. Low traffic may mean low demand or poor findability.'),
 ('Worked decision','Cedar has two fee tables with different prices. The audit records a factual conflict and asks the course owner to verify the source of truth. The IA proposal uses one maintained fee record linked from relevant courses.'),
 ('Practise','Inventory ten pages or functions. Mark each Keep, Improve, Merge, Retire or Investigate, with a reason and owner. Do not remove a page solely because it is old or rarely visited.'),
 ('Review your work','Every proposed change should connect to evidence or a clearly marked hypothesis. Resolve important factual conflicts before asking participants to use misleading content. Keep an explicit list of missing information and externally owned dependencies.')],[])
 deck('schemes','Choose an organising scheme',[
 ('Compare organising principles','This workshop compares topic, task, audience, chronology and alphabetical schemes. A site can use several at different levels. Decide which expectation each scheme supports instead of mixing incompatible categories without explanation.'),
 ('Make the distinctions concrete','| Scheme | Cedar example | Useful when |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Topic | Computing courses | People know the subject |\n| Task | Cancel a booking | An action is the goal |\n| Audience | Course tutors | Needs are role-specific |\n| Time | Events this week | Timing drives selection |\n| Alphabetical | Tutor directory | People know a name |'),
 ('Avoid unexplained mixtures','“Courses / Families / Tuesday / Help” combines topic, audience, time and support. That may create overlap. Use secondary routes or filters where they support different ways of seeking the same records.'),
 ('Worked proposal','Cedar’s primary course organisation uses subject. Filters expose schedule, level and delivery. A Family activities landing page provides a curated alternate route to relevant courses without duplicating their maintained records.'),
 ('Practise','Choose a primary scheme for fifteen inventory items. Add one justified secondary route. Write inclusion and exclusion rules for each top-level group.'),
 ('Review your work','Can you explain where a new item belongs? Are sibling labels distinct enough to compare? Use outliers to challenge the scheme and make a research plan for ambiguous boundaries.')],[])
 deck('map-model','Specify a sitemap and content model',[
 ('Document relationships','This authored workshop turns your proposal into a usable specification. A sitemap records hierarchy and important cross-links. A content model records types, fields and relationships. A task flow records steps over time; it answers a different question.'),
 ('Use a relationship table','| Item | Type | Parent | Additional route |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Courses | Category page | Home | Main navigation |\n| Pottery | Course record | Arts courses | Family activities if eligible |\n| Prepare for pottery | Guidance | Pottery | Booking confirmation |\n| Refund policy | Policy | Booking help | Relevant course pages |'),
 ('Model the record','A Course could have ID, title, summary, subject, level, dates, fee relationship, venue relationship and access information. Reusing a venue record can prevent many classes from containing outdated copies of the same directions.'),
 ('Distinguish types of relationship','“Belongs under”, “takes place at”, “requires” and “is related to” are different relationships. A sitemap alone may not capture all of them. Name the relationship before choosing how to expose it.'),
 ('Practise','Produce a sitemap table for fifteen items and a field specification for one type. Define required fields, allowed values and who owns updates.'),
 ('Review your work','Check unique IDs, orphan items, circular parent relationships, duplicate records and missing parent destinations. Test whether someone else can add a new course without asking you to explain the model.')],[])
 deck('search-workshop','Design a searchable information space',[
 ('Connect classification to retrieval','This applied workshop considers what a search experience needs: meaningful content, useful metadata, sensible result presentation and recovery. It is a design specification, not a promise that a particular search engine implements these capabilities.'),
 ('Define search behaviour','Record likely queries, synonyms, spelling variants and important vocabulary differences. Decide which content types are searchable and how restricted or retired items are excluded. Keep query logs free of unnecessary personal information.'),
 ('Use filters and facets deliberately','A facet describes a dimension such as subject, level or delivery. A filter narrows the current results. Specify whether selections combine with AND or OR, show active selections and provide a clear reset.'),
 ('Worked filter logic','A learner selects Arts OR Computing within Subject, then Beginner within Level. If dimensions combine with AND, results must be Beginner and in either selected subject. State this explicitly rather than assuming the interface makes it obvious.'),
 ('Design results and recovery','Show a descriptive title, useful summary and distinguishing context such as date, type or venue. Preserve the query when nothing matches; allow revision and clearing filters. Do not invent helpful alternatives the system cannot actually produce.'),
 ('Practise','Specify results for “evening coding”, a synonym query and an empty result after filtering. Include one ambiguous result and explain which metadata would distinguish it.'),
 ('Review your work','Check whether each result helps a learner predict relevance. Verify filter counts and combinations against real data. Test known-item searching and exploratory browsing separately; ranking quality cannot be established by a static mockup.')],[])
 deck('research-plan','Combine qualitative and quantitative evidence',[
 ('Choose the uncertainty','This workshop connects to the supplementary research guides. Qualitative work explores behaviour and reasoning; quantitative work estimates or compares measures. Neither substitutes automatically for the other.'),
 ('Recruit and protect participants','Define who belongs in the study and relevant variation. Explain consent, recording, retention and withdrawal. Avoid collecting personal information unnecessary for the research question. Keep participant details out of public project documents.'),
 ('Write a testable question','“Is our IA good?” is too broad. “Can newcomers locate cancellation conditions without selecting Membership first?” identifies an audience, destination and potential problem. Decide which observation or measure would inform a change.'),
 ('Worked mixed plan','First observe several Cedar newcomers using the current structure to understand confusion. Revise the labels, pilot a tree test, then collect a suitable larger sample if you need a comparative estimate. Check the actual interface afterwards.'),
 ('Report uncertainty honestly','Record method, participants, tasks, versions and exclusions. Separate observation, interpretation and recommendation. Report a small study as exploratory; a percentage without sample size and context can imply false precision.'),
 ('Practise','Write one qualitative question and one quantitative question for the same task. Specify the method, success rule and limitation for each.'),
 ('Review your work','“What makes this label confusing?” needs reasoning evidence. “What proportion finds the destination?” needs a sampling and measurement plan. Do not use a few comments to claim prevalence or a success rate to claim you know the cause.')],['nng-47','nng-48'])
 deck('accessibility-workshop','Check navigation across access needs',[
 ('Make access part of the design','This authored workshop applies the earlier navigation principles. A clear hierarchy still needs an operable interface. Plan meaningful landmarks, logical headings, descriptive links and readable content in the real layout.'),
 ('Specify control semantics','Use links for destinations and buttons for actions. Expose the current page and disclosure state appropriately. Ensure keyboard users can enter, operate and leave menus without a trap.'),
 ('Check focus and presentation','Keep focus visible and logical. Check that overlays close predictably and return focus to a sensible place. Enlarged text, narrow screens and persistent headers must not conceal required controls or content.'),
 ('Preserve multiple cues','Do not rely on colour, position, sound or an icon alone. Give icon controls meaningful accessible names and provide text for essential audio information. Use language versions based on audience needs and maintain terminology consistently.'),
 ('Practise','Using only the keyboard, open a section, visit a deep destination and return. Repeat at enlarged text and a narrow viewport. If possible, inspect announcements with a screen reader.'),
 ('Review your work','Record the exact action and observed failure, not “accessibility is bad”. Automated checks can help identify issues but do not establish full accessibility or legal conformance. Include people with relevant access needs in research.')],[])
 deck('migration','Plan a safe information migration',[
 ('Map old items to new destinations','This authored workshop covers change implementation. Keep a mapping from old identifiers and URLs to retained, merged, replacement or intentionally retired items. A redesign can break established routes even when the new structure is clearer.'),
 ('Handle redirects and references','For moved content, plan appropriate redirects and update internal links. Avoid redirecting unrelated old pages to Home merely to conceal missing content. Review documents, bookmarks, external services and search indexing.'),
 ('Define a cross-listing policy','Choose canonical records and document alternate routes. Check breadcrumb consistency, feeds and related-content queries. One visible page can depend on several backend relationships.'),
 ('Worked migration row','Old: /member-zone/refunds. New: /booking-help/cancellation-refunds. Decision: move verified policy, redirect the old URL, update booking confirmations, preserve the owner and test both entry routes.'),
 ('Practise','Map five old URLs, including a merger and a retirement. Define acceptance checks and a rollback plan for a high-risk move.'),
 ('Review your work','Verify destination relevance, redirect loops, access permissions, saved links and content accuracy. Test important routes before and after release. Record what to restore if the release creates a critical finding failure.')],[])
 deck('governance','Maintain the architecture after launch',[
 ('Assign decisions to owners','This authored workshop treats IA as a maintained system. Decide who may add categories, rename terms, create content types and approve cross-listing. Keep a lightweight decision record.'),
 ('Define review triggers','Review after new services, repeated support questions, failed searches, changed user language or observed task failures. A calendar review helps but does not replace responding to meaningful changes.'),
 ('Monitor outcomes','Track task success research, search failures, broken routes and support themes. Traffic and clicks need interpretation. An unused page could be unnecessary, hard to find or useful only at a rare but important moment.'),
 ('Worked decision record','Proposal: rename Enrichment to Courses. Evidence: newcomers cannot predict the contents. Owner: learning editor. Validation: tree test and observed finding task. Follow-up: review failed searches after release. Record the version and remaining uncertainty.'),
 ('Practise','Create a category-change request with its rationale, affected content, owner, research, migration actions and review trigger.'),
 ('Review your work','Could another editor apply the rule consistently? Does the proposal protect existing routes and content accuracy? Keep a method for retiring obsolete terms and mapping useful synonyms without silently losing content.')],[])
 deck('capstone','Build and evaluate your own IA',[
 ('Stage 1: define the brief','Choose a manageable information space. Name the audience, three important tasks, constraints and unknowns. Produce an inventory and audit. Keep a baseline structure and distinguish facts from assumptions.'),
 ('Stage 2: propose the architecture','Choose an organising scheme. Create a sitemap, content model and small controlled vocabulary. Write category labels, explain boundaries and justify any cross-listing. Record why competing options were rejected.'),
 ('Stage 3: research the structure','Prepare an appropriate card sort or use existing evidence. Create unbiased tree-test tasks and define acceptable destinations. Pilot, gather evidence and revise. Report sample limitations; invented practice data must remain labelled fictional.'),
 ('Stage 4: specify navigation','Choose global, local, utility and contextual routes. Plan direct-entry orientation, breadcrumbs and mobile subnavigation. Specify expanded, current, loading and failure states. Include keyboard and focus behaviour.'),
 ('Stage 5: test and maintain','Observe important tasks in a realistic prototype. Compare the result with your baseline using equivalent criteria. Produce migration, ownership and monitoring plans. Identify what remains untested.'),
 ('Self-assessment rubric','| Evidence | Check |\n| --- | --- |\n| Structure | New content has a clear home |\n| Labels | Readers predict destinations |\n| Research | Method answers the stated question |\n| Navigation | Tasks work across tested input modes |\n| Maintenance | Owners and change rules are defined |'),
 ('Review and iterate','Ask someone to challenge one category boundary and complete one task without coaching. Revise using what you observe. Your final submission is a reasoned proposal plus evidence and limitations, not simply a polished sitemap.')],[])
